WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.000
When traffic surges, responders need decisions. What is happening? What should we

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.000
do? And did it work?

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Aegis Flow brings detection, classification, response, and verification into one operations console.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.000
All traffic is synthetic. The baseline shows healthy source diversity and low

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.000
latency.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Now we launch an HTTP flood. A small group of sources repeatedly

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.000
targets the search endpoint.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.000
The detector combines rate deviation, source and path entropy, error ratio, and

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.000
connection duration to classify the attack.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.000
We stop the first attack, arm automatic mitigation, and launch a second

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.000
round. No manual rate limit is applied.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.000
After three hot ticks, the engine applies a path rate limit. Attacking

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
sources are capped at thirty requests per second.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.500
Ingress stays high while origin load falls. The yellow band shows edge

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.000
mitigation. Every action is logged.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.500
Next, a flash crowd. Hundreds of diverse users are legitimate. High traffic

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:23.000
alone does not justify blocking.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Automatic response remains armed. The engine recommends caching and leaves mitigations at

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.000
zero. An operations tool earns trust by knowing when to hold back.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.667
The AWS integration is a prototype. The repository includes a Cloud Front

00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:43.333
and WAF reference stack, plus an optional adapter for supported manual rule

00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:47.000
additions.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Offline tests check payloads, rule preservation, and locking. No live AWS deployment

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.000
is claimed.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.500
Aegis Flow makes response decisions visible. Try the browser simulation, replay an

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:09.000
incident, and explore the code.
